+# BLOCKED: exact median of a single-pass stream in O(1) memory
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires all of the following at once:
+
+- `median()` receives a one-shot generator (`feed()`), so the input can be read only once.
+- The result must be **exact**: equal to the mean of the 99,999th and 100,000th
+  values of the fully sorted 200,000-element feed.
+- Peak Python heap usage must stay below 64 KiB.
+
+There is no implementation that satisfies all three. This is a property of the
+problem, not of the current code, so I did not work around it.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Exact selection in the single-pass streaming model requires space linear in the
+stream length (Munro & Paterson, *Selection and sorting with limited storage*,
+1980: selecting the median in `p` passes needs `Θ(n^(1/p))` storage, which is
+`Θ(n)` for one pass). Intuitively, to return a middle order statistic exactly,
+an algorithm must still be able to distinguish inputs whose middle elements
+differ. Any value it has discarded can be replaced by an adversary with a value
+that moves the middle, so a constant number of retained candidates cannot be
+correct for all inputs.
+
+Bits of precision do not create a loophole here:
+
+- Approximate streaming quantile algorithms (P², t-digest, KLL, reservoir
+  sampling) use bounded memory but return approximate answers. The test demands
+  exact equality, including the exact mean of the two middle values, so they
+  cannot pass.
+- Binary-searching the 64-bit float space would need many passes over the
+  input; `feed()` is a generator and is already consumed after one pass.
+- External merge sort on temp files would keep RAM bounded, but it trades the
+  O(1)-memory requirement for O(n) disk and is a workaround for the stated
+  requirement rather than a streaming algorithm. It is listed as an option
+  below, not implemented.
+
+## Measured evidence
+
+With the exact (sorted) implementation on the test feed (`n=200_000`):
+
+```
+result:      120.12043743895683   (exactly equals the test's `want`)
+peak bytes:  7,224,176            (~7.05 MiB)
+limit:          65,536            (64 KiB)
+```
+
+The value assertion now passes; only `assertLess(peak, 64 * 1024)` fails, by a
+factor of ~110.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+- Even-count median bug: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns `2.5` (mean of the
+  middle two) instead of `3`. Odd counts and `ValueError` on empty input are
+  unchanged.
+- All other tests pass: `test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`,
+  `test_generator_input`.
+- `test_constant_memory` fails on the memory assertion only. It was left
+  untouched so the unsatisfiable requirement stays visible.
+
+## Options for infra
+
+1. **Relax exactness**: use a bounded-memory streaming quantile sketch
+   (t-digest/KLL/P²). Memory becomes O(1) or O(log n), but results are
+   approximate.
+2. **Allow replay**: pass a re-iterable sequence instead of a generator and
+   permit multiple passes. Exact selection then needs only O(1) RAM (many
+   scans over the value space).
+3. **Allow external storage**: chunked external merge sort gives exact results
+   with O(1) RAM, at the cost of O(n) temp disk and extra I/O.
+4. **Accept O(n) RAM**: keep the current exact sort; here it costs ~36 bytes
+   per value (~7 MiB for 200k values), which is what causes the worker OOM on
+   large feeds.
